    "title": "Bourbon County High School program boosts student success with mental health aid - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Allie Root ,\n1 hours ago\nPARIS, Ky. ( FOX 56 ) — As conversations around mental health grow louder across the nation, one local school system is stepping up to help its students succeed in and out of the classroom.\nBourbon County Schools is introducing a new program to help students facing hard times.\nLATEST KENTUCKY NEWS: Bourbon County High School program boosts student success with mental health aid Adair County man arrested after allegedly sharing child exploitation images online Kentucky Chamber shares new reports with lawmakers to chart path on child care Life is unpredictable, and the pressure of academics, sports, and family life can weigh on young people.\n“We know if the students are not mentally okay, they are not going to be academically okay,” said district mental health coordinator Paula Thompson.\nThat’s why Thompson has launched a program called Handle with Care, a simple, confidential way to give students extra support.\nParents can fill out a Google form to let school staff know their child is facing a tough time and may need extra patience and care while learning.\nLATEST KENTUCKY LISTS AND RANKINGS: MAP: How much of the $815M Powerball jackpot would you actually keep in Kentucky? A mansion for under $1M? It’s still the norm in this Kentucky city How tick-infested is Kentucky? Data shows surging levels of tick-borne illness “It could be that your kiddo had a bad night the night before,” she said. “It could be that they didn’t do well in their soccer game that night. It could be because you have a major crisis going on.”\nKimberlee McElroy, a parent, said teachers don’t always realize how many challenges students face outside of school and how serious they can be.\n“If anything—anything traumatic or overwhelming—has happened outside of school, they bring it the next day,” said McElroy. “And that can definitely impact their performance in the classroom.”\nSo, Thompson said this program allows parents and school staff to have open communication, helping to ensure the well-being of their children.\n“It just gives us an opportunity to keep an extra eye out, maybe give an extra smile, and sometimes even give a little bit of space to that child,” said Thompson.\nLatest central Kentucky weather forecast McElroy said it’s nice to know her child is not alone.\n“That makes me feel really good as a parent to know that they care about my child,” she said.\nThompson noted that success in and even beyond the classroom starts with feeling safe and supported.\n“Our theme this year is Run Your Race, and that means run their own race, which means I have to do everything I can to support them to get to the finish line. And that’s exactly what we intend to do.”\nThe program is currently available only at the district’s high school, but Thompson said the goal is to expand it districtwide soon.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to FOX 56 News.",
